6. Ethical Boundaries

The same technique can serve a stage trick, an interactive story, or a real decision. The boundary depends on what people agreed to, what they reasonably expect to control, and what the choice can cost them.

Setting	Boundary
Stage mentalism	Invite people to participate in an illusion and make it easy to decline. The method can stay secret. Permission to participate covers the performance and its agreed scope. Ask separately before using sensitive personal information or recording or sharing someone’s participation.
Pantheon encounters	A story can hide clues, delay a reveal, and converge on a shared scene. If you tell players a choice changes what they can do, give it that effect. In The Gate That Remembers, every path opens the gate, and each clue unlocks a different action inside.
Money Bin experiments	Tell participants they’re taking part in a trick or a design exercise. Keep the surprise intact during the run. Explain what you varied afterward and ask permission before retaining or sharing identifiable responses. Record observations and inferences separately.
Real decisions	Make material options and consequences clear before commitment. If an action affects someone’s money, privacy, access, or obligations, state what it does before they take it. Use the same meaning whichever option they pick.

For each encounter, record three things alongside the branch map: what participants agreed to, what their choices actually control, and what the encounter learns or retains about them.